# Environment template for the load-test stack.
#
# Copy to services/test/.env, then fill in the REQUIRED values below:
#   cp services/test/.env.example services/test/.env
#
# ── How this file relates to services/docker/.env ──────────────────────────
#
# compose.yaml here composes ../docker/compose.yaml, and Compose loads BOTH
# .env files. Measured behaviour, not inference:
#
#   - a key set only in services/docker/.env      -> resolves here too
#   - a key set only in services/test/.env        -> resolves here
#   - a key set in both                           -> services/test/.env wins
#
# So this file only needs the REQUIRED entries plus whatever you deliberately
# want to differ from the development stack. It does NOT need to restate every
# variable the development stack already defines.
#
# The consequence worth knowing: if services/docker/.env is missing, this stack
# still fails on SMISKI_HOST_IP and CURSOR_SECRET, because those are declared
# with the `:?` error form in the included file. Set them in one place or the
# other.

# ── REQUIRED — the stack fails to start without these ──────────────────────

# Host LAN IP that LiveKit advertises in ICE candidates. Loopback does not work
# under rootless Docker. Inherited from services/docker/.env if set there.
SMISKI_HOST_IP=192.168.1.10

# HMAC key for opaque pagination cursors, read by both `tenant` and `meet`.
# Inherited from services/docker/.env if set there.
CURSOR_SECRET=change-me-cursor-secret-must-be-at-least-32-chars

# ── TURN relay (Coturn) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Shared secret for Coturn's `use-auth-secret` mode. THE SAME VALUE must reach
# both Coturn's `static-auth-secret` and LiveKit's rtc.turn_servers[].secret,
# which compose.yaml wires from this one variable — LiveKit derives the
# time-limited username/credential pair itself, so no static user is stored.
#
# A mismatch does not fail at startup. It surfaces only as a TURN allocation
# failure at join time, which reads like a relay outage rather than a
# configuration error, so change it in one place only: here.
TURN_SHARED_SECRET=change-me-turn-shared-secret

# Address and port LiveKit advertises to clients for the relay.
#
# THIS IS COTURN'S STATIC IP, not the `coturn` service name. The TC-01 browser
# sits on the client network and reaches the media network only through nat-gw;
# it cannot resolve the `coturn` service name from there, but it can route the
# pinned address. LiveKit advertises whatever is set here to the client, so an
# IP is what lets Leg 2's relay allocation succeed from behind the NAT. The
# harness offers the same address as its STUN server for Leg 1.
TURN_HOST=10.77.0.12
TURN_PORT=3478

# Authentication realm Coturn presents. With use-auth-secret the realm is not a
# credential and any stable value works, but it must not be empty — Coturn
# requires one to answer a long-term-credential challenge at all.
TURN_REALM=smiski.test

# Lifetime of each derived TURN credential, in seconds. Must comfortably exceed
# the longest measured session — TC-02 runs 15 minutes — or the credential
# expires mid-call and the relay drops.
TURN_CREDENTIAL_TTL=86400

# ── LiveKit metrics ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Port for LiveKit's Prometheus listener, serving /metrics. The development
# stack leaves this unset, so LiveKit publishes nothing there; the overlay
# enables it because TC-02/TC-03 read participant, track, packet-loss, jitter
# and round-trip-time series from it.
#
# Not published to the host: only Prometheus reads it, over the stack network.
#
# CHANGING THIS REQUIRES A SECOND EDIT. Prometheus does not expand environment
# variables in its configuration file, so observability/prometheus.yml carries
# the port as a literal `livekit-server:6789`. Measured: with the interpolated
# form, `promtool check config` still reports SUCCESS while the running server
# resolves the target to the literal text and the job never scrapes. Change the
# port here and in observability/prometheus.yml together, or LiveKit metrics
# silently stop arriving and TC-02/TC-03 lose their server-side series.
LIVEKIT_PROMETHEUS_PORT=6789

# ── Browser QoS harness ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Host port for the static harness page that joins a room with a pasted token,
# lets ICE choose its own path and samples RTCStatsReport to CSV. Chosen outside
# the development stack's published range to avoid a collision. Used by the
# host-browser legs (TC-02/TC-03); the TC-01 NAT browser opens the page by the
# harness's in-stack address instead.
HARNESS_PORT=8090

# ── TC-01 NAT client (containerised browser behind nat-gw) ─────────────────
#
# TC-01 drives a real desktop Chromium that sits behind a NAT gateway, so its
# connection to LiveKit crosses a genuine NAT and ICE picks the path itself:
# a server-reflexive/peer-reflexive candidate for Leg 1 (direct traversal) and
# a TURN relay for Leg 2 once `smiski test impair blocked-udp --service nat-gw`
# drops UDP in the gateway. The operator drives it by hand over noVNC.

# Container image for that browser. Defaults to :latest for a first run; PIN it
# to a specific Chromium version tag for reproducible measurement, the same
# reason every tool image in scripts/src/lib/docker.ts is pinned.
BROWSER_IMAGE=lscr.io/linuxserver/chromium:latest

# Host port for the browser's noVNC console. Off the inherited range because the
# inherited Grafana already publishes host 3000.
BROWSER_VNC_PORT=3010

# Timezone for the browser container. Cosmetic; affects only its clock.
TZ=UTC

# ── Locally signed Forge Invocation Tokens ─────────────────────────────────
#
# Atlassian signs the real FIT and it cannot be minted locally, so the test
# stack verifies tokens against a locally generated key instead. Generate the
# material before starting the stack:
#
#   pnpm --dir scripts smiski test keygen
#
# Envoy reads the JWKS at STARTUP and also at `--mode validate` time. A missing
# file fails validation with `Invalid path`, so keygen must run first.

# Directory holding generated key material. Gitignored — see .gitignore in this
# directory. The private key never leaves it and is never mounted into Envoy.
TEST_KEY_DIR=./keys

# Public JSON Web Key Set, mounted read-only into Envoy and referenced by
# local_jwks.filename in envoy/envoy.yaml. Safe to commit in principle, but kept
# gitignored with the rest of the generated material.
TEST_JWKS_PATH=./keys/jwks.json

# RSA private key used to sign tokens. NEVER COMMIT THIS. It stays on the host
# and is read only by `smiski test token`.
TEST_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=./keys/private.pem

# Key identifier written into the JWKS and into each token header, so a rotated
# key is distinguishable from the one Envoy currently trusts.
TEST_KEY_ID=smiski-test-key

# ── Mock Jira ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Deliberately NOT set here.
#
# The gateway's project-permission lookups are redirected at the in-stack mock
# by compose.yaml, which writes the value literally. That is not a stylistic
# choice: services/docker/.env already sets JIRA_API_BASE, and both .env files
# load, so a ${JIRA_API_BASE:-...} default would never apply. Setting the key
# here as well would be inert — the literal in compose.yaml wins — while
# suggesting this is the place to change the redirect.
#
# The failure mode that pattern produces is silent: the gateway would call the
# real Atlassian API, time out after 2 seconds and return empty permissions,
# surfacing only as latency in the exact figure TC-04 measures. To point the
# gateway somewhere else, edit compose.yaml.

# ── Observability ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The exporters and collectors start only under the profile:
#   docker compose -f services/test/compose.yaml --profile observability up -d
#
# This stack adds livekit-redis-exporter to the development stack's four, so
# both cache instances are separate scrape targets — TC-05 sets a memory
# threshold on "the Redis container" while the stack runs two.

GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin # gitleaks:allow

# Host paths the in-process cAdvisor exporter reads, both read-only. Correct for
# Docker Engine on Linux; on Docker Desktop neither path exists on the host and
# the per-container CPU and memory panels stay empty.
CADVISOR_CGROUP_ROOT=/sys/fs/cgroup
CADVISOR_DOCKER_ROOT=/var/lib/docker

# ── Host port note ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Host ports are inherited unchanged from the development stack and are
# hardcoded there (30000, 9901, 8281-8284, 9094, 7880-7882, 3000, 9090, 3100,
# 12345). The two stacks therefore cannot run simultaneously as written, even
# though their volumes and networks are fully separate. Stop one before starting
# the other.
